//! The `block_drops` hooks and helpers: declarations the mod API generator reads (see `hooks/mod.rs`).

pub trait Hooks {
    /// NeoForge's `BlockDropsEvent`: a block `state` broke at `pos` and `Block.dropResources` has built the item entities `drops` it will spawn, but nothing is in the world yet. `breaker` is the breaking player's UUID (none for a break the level makes: a piston, water, a neighbour update, `/setblock destroy`) and `tool` borrows the stack they used lazily as [`ItemStackView`] (none when no tool; call [`ItemStackView::project`] to copy components on demand). `breaker_entity` is the entity that broke the block (NeoForge's `getBreaker()`): the player, or the mob whose own break of this block is running (a ravager's, a wither's). It is none when no entity broke it: a piston, water, a neighbour update, `/setblock destroy` and explosions. `block_entity` is the original break subject's resident block entity (NeoForge's `getBlockEntity()`), never a replacement at the same position. When the original was removed or kept only as packed chunk data, `block_entity_data` lends its read-only payload (including `CustomName`, without identity or position). That includes player breaks, packed level-side or mob breaks, and mob breaks whose `loot_drops` listener removes or replaces the original. Explosions and blocks without an entity give neither field. `drops` is a live [`PreparedBlockDrops`] view of the list `Block.popResource` built: each stack with the position, motion and pickup delay its item entity will have. Ordinary breaks prepare these before the event; explosions defer random fields until observed or joined. Reading a drop's item key, count, damage or entity fields does not project component patches; edit entries in place via [`PreparedDropMut`] to preserve each prepared drop's RNG, or copy/add/replace entries via owned [`ItemDrop`] captures. Stack writes are validated per edit with [`StackWriteError`] so an invalid edit leaves all other drops, `experience` and cancel state intact. Edit `experience`, the points `spawnAfterBreak` would award at the block's centre (ores, sculk, spawners; silk touch has already zeroed it, as NeoForge's description says, "the true value, after enchantments"). Set `cancel` and nothing spawns: no drops, no experience, and none of `spawnAfterBreak`'s other effects (an infested block's silverfish); a bee nest's bees are `playerDestroy`'s, not `spawnAfterBreak`'s, and still come out. With the `block_drops` rule off, positions are still drawn but no item entities are constructed, so `drops` starts empty. No items or experience spawn after the event. It fires on the player's break, a mob's block break that drops loot, explosions and every level-side `Block.dropResources` (a piston breaking a block, water washing one away, a removed support). Player, explosion and mob block loot passes through `loot_drops` first; level-side breaks are not asked `loot_drops`. Explosion events run after the blast has removed its blocks and merged their loot, before any items spawn, with no breaker or tool and zero initial experience. Each block's event owns only its share of a merged stack, at that entity's prepared position and velocity; cancellation cannot remove another block's share. Unchanged shares rejoin the original merged entity. Compatible order edits across blocks all carry through to joins. An entry edited in place keeps its placement relative to the other entries in its list without fixing their order relative to each other. Earlier blocks take precedence only for conflicting edits; among ready groups, vanilla order wins. Each shared group joins only once. Even a block with no loot gets an event (TNT that primes instead does not). Without edits or reads of random fields, an explosion draws and joins each group before drawing for the next. Reading position or velocity prepares that field in vanilla group order through the observed group. An item or experience edit prepares the original captures before item joins. Already admitted items still join if an experience orb's join listener disables the `block_drops` rule. It does not fire for dispensers or a block's own loot-table drop through another path. It runs inside the level's write, so the level host reads but cannot write or borrow the level random. Authoritative servers only. Replaces `block_exp`, whose catalog row named no NeoForge event (`none`) and fired only on the level-side path: the amount is `experience` here. Diverges from upstream: `block_exp` is removed into this hook.
    #[notes = r#"## Block drops

- **`block_drops(level, dimension, pos, state, breaker, breaker_entity, block_entity, block_entity_data, tool, drops, experience, cancel)`** (NeoForge's `BlockDropsEvent`): `Block.dropResources` built the item entities for a broken block and nothing has spawned yet. `tool` borrows the breaking stack lazily (`ItemStackView`: call `project()` to copy components on demand; none when no tool). `drops` is a live `PreparedBlockDrops` view: inspect scalar stack/entity fields without projecting components, mutate entries in place via `PreparedDropMut` to preserve each prepared drop's RNG, or add/replace/copy via owned `ItemDrop` captures (stack, position, velocity, pickup delay, and the item entity's thrower, target, no-gravity and starting age); stack writes validate individually with `StackWriteError` so a rejected edit leaves all other drops, `experience` and cancel state intact. Edit `experience` or set `cancel` for no drops, no experience and no `spawnAfterBreak` effects (an infested block's silverfish; a bee nest's bees are `playerDestroy`'s and still come out). `breaker` is the player's UUID, none for a level-side break; `breaker_entity` is the entity that broke it (the player, or the mob whose own break runs; none for a piston, water, a neighbour update, `/setblock destroy` and explosions). `block_entity` is present only while the original break subject remains resident, never for a replacement. `block_entity_data` lends the original payload (including `CustomName`, without identity or position) if stored only in the chunk or removed before the callback, including mob breaks whose loot listener replaces the original. Explosions and blocks without an entity give neither field. It fires on the player's break, mob block breaks that drop loot, explosions and every level-side `Block.dropResources`. Ordinary breaks draw positions and motions before the event. Explosions prepare a position or velocity only when read (through that group in vanilla order) or when joining; item or experience edits prepare the original captures before joins. With `block_drops` off, positions are still drawn but the initial list is empty and no items or XP spawn. It runs inside a level write, so it gets the read-only level host. Player, mob and explosion block loot still runs `loot_drops` first; a level-side break is not asked `loot_drops`. Not fired for dispensers or other loot-table drops. Explosions start with zero experience, but a listener may award it. Already admitted items still join if an experience orb's join listener disables the `block_drops` rule. Their events run after the blast removes the blocks and merges loot, before items spawn. Each block owns only its contribution to a merged entity, with that entity's prepared position and velocity: cancel removes only that share. Unchanged shares rejoin the original merged entity; compatible order edits across blocks all carry through to item joins. Editing a field places that share relative to its peers without fixing those peers' order. Earlier blocks win only when later orders conflict; otherwise ready groups follow vanilla order, each joining once. Empty-loot blocks still fire; TNT that primes instead does not. Without edits or reads of random fields, the vanilla pop and join sequence is unchanged, including the effect of intervening join callbacks. Tick-time effect: explosions add a `listened` branch per block; contribution tracking allocates only with listeners. Mob breaks use the same item construction and XP sampling as other block breaks. Level-side breaks and explosion loot fire it too. Diverges from upstream: `block_exp` is removed into `experience` (its catalog row named no NeoForge event)."#]
    #[hook(
        receiver = "read",
        dispatch = "api_carrier",
        side = "server",
        contract = "cancel",
        out = "cancel",
        neoforge = "BlockDropsEvent",
        cancellable = true,
        version = 3,
        target = "sunrise_client::game::native_hooks::block_drops::GameLevel<'_>::native_block_drops"
    )]
    fn block_drops(
        dimension: LevelDimension,
        pos: BlockPos,
        state: BlockState,
        breaker: Option<u128>,
        breaker_entity: Option<EntityRef>,
        block_entity: Option<BlockEntityRef>,
        block_entity_data: Option<&Compound>,
        tool: Option<ItemStackView<'_>>,
        drops: PreparedBlockDrops<'_>,
        experience: &mut u32,
        cancel: &mut bool,
    );
}
